text:
**Case: Pasquariello v. Italy** (Application no. 57962/22), European Court of Human Rights, First Section, judgment delivered 16 May 2024.

The applicant, Gianpiero Pasquariello, born in 1972, lodged an application against Italy on 9 December 2022. The Italian Government were given notice of the application. The case was considered by a Committee composed of Judges Paczolay, Felici, and Sabato.

The applicant’s central complaint was the non-enforcement or delayed enforcement of domestic decisions. He also raised other complaints under the Convention. The domestic law relevant to the case concerns the Pinto Act, as set out in *Cocchiarella v. Italy* and *Di Giuseppe v. Italy*.

The key facts are contained in the appended table. Between 25 May 2017 and 9 December 2020, nine domestic decisions were issued in the applicant’s favour by various Italian courts and tribunals:

1. Perugia Court of Appeal, R.G. 5390/2012, 25 May 2017;
2. Salerno Court of Appeal, R.G. 929/2017, 27 February 2018;
3. Rome Court of Appeal, R.G. 56726/2012, 18 May 2018;
4. Naples Court of Appeal, R.G. 1793/2018, 18 October 2018;
5. Lazio Regional Administrative Tribunal, R.G. 02613/2019, 19 February 2020;
6. Naples Court of Appeal, R.G. 2408/2019, 20 February 2020;
7. Naples Court of Appeal, R.G. 810/2020, 4 June 2020;
8. Naples Court of Appeal, R.G. 941/2020, 30 June 2020;
9. Naples Court of Appeal, R.G. 2056/2020, 9 December 2020.

Each domestic decision ordered the Ministry of Justice and the Ministry of Economy and Finance to pay lawyer legal fees (“avvocato antistatario”). These decisions therefore imposed payment obligations on Italian public authorities in favour of the applicant in his capacity as a lawyer entitled to recover fees.

According to the table, the start date of the non-enforcement period for each decision corresponds to the date of the relevant domestic decision. The end date for every decision is recorded as “pending.” Consequently, as of the proceedings before the European Court, enforcement remained outstanding for all nine decisions. The non-enforcement periods were already lengthy:

- Perugia decision: more than 6 years, 6 months, and 10 days;
- Salerno decision: more than 5 years, 9 months, and 8 days;
- Rome decision: more than 5 years, 6 months, and 17 days;
- Naples decision (18 October 2018): more than 5 years, 1 month, and 17 days;
- Lazio TAR decision: more than 3 years, 9 months, and 16 days;
- Naples decision (20 February 2020): more than 3 years, 9 months, and 15 days;
- Naples decision (4 June 2020): more than 3 years, 6 months, and 1 day;
- Naples decision (30 June 2020): more than 3 years, 5 months, and 5 days;
- Naples decision (9 December 2020): more than 2 years, 11 months, and 26 days.

The alleged violation(s) therefore arise from Italy’s failure to enforce, or its delayed enforcement of, final domestic decisions awarding the applicant legal fees. The repeated and continuing non-enforcement across multiple decisions, all involving the same public authorities, forms the factual core of the complaint. The applicant also raised additional Convention complaints, but the principal issue concerns the unresolved execution of these domestic orders. The judgment is final but may be subject to editorial revision.
